A family with more than one additive predictor fits one forest per predictor.
Nuisance parameters are drawn alongside the trees and reported in \code{fit$aux}.
\subsection{Links the Engine Does Not Compile}{

The links listed above are the ones the sampler evaluates in compiled code.
Any other link is accepted for \code{gaussian()}, \code{binomial()}, \code{poisson()} and
\code{Beta()}, and applied from R by composing the caller's inverse link with the
family's own, with the chain rule carrying the derivatives back. So
\code{binomial("cauchit")} works, as does any link object of the kind
\code{\link[stats:make.link]{stats::make.link()}} returns. It costs a call into R for every leaf the
sampler visits, and the leaf prior scale is calibrated for the compiled link.

Two single-predictor families are exceptions. \code{negbin()} takes \code{"log"} alone,
so a link given to it is an error rather than a composition. \code{\link[stats:Gamma]{stats::Gamma()}}
accepts any link and fits none of them but \code{"log"}: every other link is
dropped with a message, because the ones base R offers have inverses that go
non-positive over part of the line and the additive predictor is
unconstrained. \code{custom_family()} is the route to a gamma response on another
link.

A composed link whose inverse has a restricted range (\code{poisson("identity")},
\code{poisson("sqrt")}) will give non-finite densities for some predictors. Those
proposals are rejected rather than breaking the chain, but they are wasted
work and the fit is worse for it, so \code{bartisan()} says so when it starts.
Prefer links whose inverse is defined on the whole line.

The families with more than one additive predictor, or whose link enters
somewhere other than a single mean (\code{ordinal()}, \code{multinomial()}, the
accelerated failure time families, \code{gaussian_ls()}, the zero-inflated
families and \code{ordbeta()}), take only their listed links. \code{custom_family()} is
the way to reach anything else.
}

\subsection{If No Family Is Given}{

\code{family} may be omitted, in which case it is read off the response's type and
the choice reported with a message; \code{\link[=bartisan]{bartisan()}} tabulates the lookup, since
\code{family} is its argument. Two things about it are worth knowing here, because
both are deliberate rather than oversights: a count is \emph{not} given \code{poisson()}
and a numeric response taking two values other than 0 and 1 is \emph{not} given
\code{binomial()}, either being a modeling decision rather than a reading of the
response's type. \code{dpm()} cannot take prior weights, so a weighted fit with no
family named is an error rather than a silent substitution.
}

\subsection{What to Know Before Reading the Output}{

\code{ordinal()} accepts a numeric response as well as an ordered factor, taking
its sorted unique values as the categories, and that is \strong{a method rather than
a fallback}: the cutpoints absorb the marginal distribution of the response
and the forest explains only the ordering, so nothing is assumed about the
error distribution and the model for \eqn{P(Y \le y \mid x)} is invariant to
any monotone transformation of the response. Predict with
\code{type = "mean"}. Bin the response onto twenty-odd quantiles first: one
cutpoint per distinct value costs 73 seconds against 2.7 for \code{gaussian()} at
1000 observations, and twenty-five bins was both sixteen times faster and
slightly more accurate. See the vignette.

\code{ordinal()} uses the cumulative-link parameterization of \pkgfun{MASS}{polr},
in which \eqn{P(Y \le k) = F(c_k - \eta)}, so larger values of the additive
predictor shift mass toward higher categories. Only the differences
\eqn{c_k - \eta_i} are identified, so one location has to be pinned: with three
or more categories the draws are reported in the chart where \strong{the additive
predictor has mean zero over the fitted sample and every cutpoint is free},
which is the chart \code{polr()} reports in when its predictors are centered. With
exactly two categories the single boundary is folded into the intercept
instead, so a two-category response is exactly binary regression with the
matching link and on the same scale. \code{cut1} is therefore a free parameter
rather than a constant zero, which is a change from earlier versions.

\code{multinomial()} by default fits one forest per category and leaves the model
unidentified, since adding any function of the predictors to every category's
forest leaves the probabilities alone. This is the parameterization of Murray
(2021), whose point is that the prior is then symmetric in the categories;
every identified quantity is recovered from the draws. Passing \code{reference}
instead pins that category at zero and fits one fewer forest, giving log odds
against it.

\code{multinomial("probit")} lets the latent utilities correlate, which a
multinomial logit cannot express at all. \eqn{\Sigma} is normalized by the
trace constraint \eqn{\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma) = C} (Burgette and Nordheim 2012),
and its lower triangle appears in \code{fit$aux} as \code{sigma11}, \code{sigma21} and so on.
\strong{Those correlations are weakly identified: read the fitted probabilities, not
the covariance.} They enter the likelihood only through orthant probabilities
of a distribution whose location is a sum of trees, so a flexible mean absorbs
much of the dependence they are meant to measure. At 900 observations a true
correlation of zero came back as -0.57, and posterior intervals ran up to 1.07
wide on a parameter confined to \eqn{(-1, 1)}; at 3000 observations the
posterior tracks the truth to within about 0.2. Two further consequences: the
likelihood has \strong{no closed form}, so it and every category probability are
simulated with \code{replicates} draws, and \code{augment} does not apply, because the
latent variables are the model rather than a rewriting of it. The sampler is
Algorithm P2 of Xu et al. (2025).

\code{dpm()} is not a distribution but a way of not choosing one. It is DPMBART
(George et al. 2019): a numeric response with the sum of trees for its mean, as
\code{gaussian()} has, and a Dirichlet process mixture of normals for its errors
instead of a single normal, so the error distribution comes out as whatever
mixture the data ask for. \code{\link[=error_density]{error_density()}} gives that density, which is the
object the method exists to produce.

\strong{It is the family to reach for by default on a numeric response}, because it
does not pay for its flexibility: on normal errors, where \code{gaussian()} is
exactly right, it came out slightly ahead on both held-out error and log score
at the same time to one decimal place, and on heavy-tailed, skewed and bimodal
errors it was ahead by a great deal (on bimodal errors at a thousand
observations, 0.050 against 0.154 in held-out RMSE, a factor of three, at the
same time to a tenth of a second). So it is the family a numeric response gets
when none is named. The reasons to prefer \code{gaussian()} are not statistical:
it takes \strong{prior weights}, which \code{dpm()} refuses, and it reports one
interpretable \code{sigma} where \code{dpm()} has a mixture. It is also faster, by 1.4
times at a thousand observations. The vignette has the comparison.

Two things to know about \code{dpm()} itself. \strong{It does not buy
heteroskedasticity}: the error distribution is flexible but it is the same
distribution at every \eqn{x}, and \code{gaussian_ls()} is the family for a
spread that depends on the predictors. And \strong{the additive predictor is the
conditional mean}, as it is for \code{gaussian()}: nothing in the model forces the
mixture to be centered, so the sampler works in a chart where only the sum of
the predictor and the error mean is identified, but reporting is done in the
chart where the mixture has mean zero and the whole conditional mean sits on
the predictor. \code{type = "link"} and \code{type = "response"} therefore agree
exactly, and \code{fit$aux} reports the shift that was taken out as \code{center} rather
than an error mean, which is zero by construction. Prior weights are refused,
since a weight would have to be a multiplicity in the Dirichlet process.

The gamma family puts the forest on the log mean and draws the shape, which
acts as the inverse dispersion; it does \emph{not} regress the shape on the
predictors. \code{negbin()} and \code{ordbeta()} take \code{theta} and \code{phi} to fix their
equivalents, and the gamma shape has no such argument because a caller who
knows it is rare. \strong{The link is where the care is needed.} Only \code{log} is
compiled, and the base R default of \code{inverse} is the worst case for this
sampler: its inverse maps a negative predictor to a negative mean, whose log
is not a number, so the proposal is rejected, dozens of times per fit.
Measured on 600 observations and 50 trees, \code{stats::Gamma()} took 7.2 seconds
against 3.8 for \code{Gamma("log")} and fitted the mean slightly worse. So write
the link, or name the family as the string \code{"Gamma"}, which resolves to the
log link; base R's own function is left as base R defines it, so that
attaching \pkg{bartisan} cannot change what \code{glm()} does. Any composed link
whose inverse has a restricted range is reported when the fit starts.

The accelerated failure time families expect a right-censored response,
supplied either as a \pkgfun{survival}{Surv} object or as a two-column matrix
of times and event indicators. They model \eqn{\log T = \eta + \sigma\epsilon}
with \eqn{\epsilon} a standard smallest extreme value, logistic or normal
variate respectively, giving
Weibull, log-logistic and log-normal survival times.

\strong{A contrast in the predictor is a log time ratio in all of them, and in
\code{dpm_aft()} too}, because with \eqn{\epsilon} independent of \eqn{x} every
quantile and both means of \eqn{T} scale by \eqn{e^{\Delta\eta}} whatever
shape the error has. What differs is what \eqn{e^{\eta}} is on its own, each
family pinning its error's location differently: the median of \eqn{T} for
\code{loglogistic_aft()} and \code{lognormal_aft()}, the geometric mean for \code{dpm_aft()},
and the Weibull scale for \code{weibull_aft()}. Contrasts are unaffected by any of
that; \code{vignette("survival")} tabulates the levels and measures the difference
between them.

\code{weibull_aft()} is also the one family whose predictor carries a log \emph{hazard}
ratio, of \eqn{-\Delta\eta/\sigma}, alongside its log time ratio. That is a
property of the smallest extreme value error rather than of the structural part it shares with
the other three: it is the only error making an accelerated failure time model
a proportional hazards model as well.

\code{ph()} is the proportional hazards alternative, with a piecewise-constant
baseline: \eqn{\lambda(t \mid x) = \lambda_0(t)\exp(r(x))}, so its predictor
is a log \emph{hazard} ratio and the baseline is free to take any shape rather than
the monotone one a Weibull imposes. \code{num_bins} sets how many pieces, with the
edges at evenly spaced quantiles of the observed times; the default is about
the cube root of the sample size. The bin hazards are drawn from their exact
gamma conditionals and reported as \code{lambda1}, \code{lambda2}, ... in \code{fit$aux},
together with the rate of their own prior. The predictor and the baseline are
identified only jointly, so the baseline carries the level and the predictor
is reported centered on it.

Cox's \emph{partial} likelihood is what cannot be used here: it couples
observations through risk sets and so does not decompose into a sum over the
observations reaching a leaf. The full likelihood of the piecewise-exponential
model does decompose, and it approaches the partial likelihood as the bins
shrink, which is how \code{ph()} reaches proportional hazards without it.

\strong{\code{num_bins} is not a modeling decision, and its default should be left
alone.} It is exposed for checking that rather than for tuning: swept over a
sixty-fold range the estimates move by less than the spread between
replicates, with no trend. What the bin count does change is the effective
number of parameters, which grows with it, and that is what makes the default
matter for \link[=bartisan-interop]{loo()} and \code{waic()} rather than for the
estimates: one parameter per event time would leave each observation's density
inflated by a parameter only it informs, and leave-one-out unable to do its
job. \code{vignette("survival")} has the sweep.

The three differ in cost, though not enough to decide a model on.
\code{lognormal_aft()} and \code{loglogistic_aft()} impute each censored failure time
above its censoring time, which makes their targets quadratic and is worth a
large multiple of the speed; \code{weibull_aft()} needs no imputation because its
likelihood already has a form the sampler can collapse to a single pass, but
only under hard rules, which makes it the slowest of the three at the default
gate.

\code{dpm_aft()} is the accelerated failure time model with the error distribution
estimated rather than assumed: \eqn{\log T = m(x) + W} with \eqn{W} a
Dirichlet process mixture of normals constrained to mean zero, and censored
log-times imputed. It is \code{dpm()}'s error model with censoring, so its predictor
is the conditional mean of \eqn{\log T} and a contrast in it is a log time
ratio exactly as for the three parametric families above, \code{error_density()}
reports the fitted error density, and prior weights are refused for the same
reason \code{dpm()} refuses them. Following Henderson, Louis, Rosner and Varadhan
(2020).

Reach for it when the shape of the error is in doubt and there is no reason to
assert one. Measured against a two-component error it was worth 210 held-out
log points and a third of the error in \eqn{S(t \mid x)} over the best
fixed-error family; against a log-normal error, where \code{lognormal_aft()} is
correctly specified, the two were within 0.1 log points of each other. So it
gains where the assumption would have been wrong and costs nothing where it
would have been right, which is the property \code{dpm()} has against \code{gaussian()}.

\code{gaussian_ls()} regresses the mean and the log standard deviation of a
normal response on separate forests, so the variance is an unrestricted
function of the predictors.

\code{Gamma_ls()} does the same for a gamma response: the first forest is the log
mean, exactly as \code{Gamma("log")}'s is, and the second is the log dispersion, so
the shape is \code{exp(-log_dispersion)} at each observation rather than one value
drawn for the whole sample. What it relaxes is the assumption that the
coefficient of variation is constant, which is what a gamma with a single
shape asserts, and giving its second forest an intercept-only formula puts
that assumption back; see "Several additive predictors" above.

Note that the mean forest is quadratic in neither family, but it takes the
same cheap exponential form \code{Gamma("log")}'s does, while the dispersion forest
takes the general path, so the cost sits in the second forest and it is worth
giving that one fewer trees.

\code{zi_poisson()} and \code{zi_negbin()} are zero-inflated counts. Both parts get their
own forest: the first predictor is the log mean of the count component and the
second the log odds that an observation is a structural zero, so the
excess-zero mechanism is free to depend on the predictors. The two are
reported as the \code{count} and \code{zero} predictors.

\code{Beta()} is beta regression for a response strictly inside the unit interval:
a forest on the link of the mean, and a precision drawn alongside it. A
response \emph{at} either endpoint has no beta density, so it is an error rather
than something to nudge inward.

\code{ordbeta()} is the ordered beta regression of Kubinec (2023), for a response on
the closed unit interval with point masses at zero and one. One predictor
drives both the probability of landing on an endpoint, through a pair of
cutpoints as in an ordinal model, and the mean of the beta density in between.
Because the predictor also enters the beta mean it is identified, so unlike
\code{ordinal()} both cutpoints are drawn.

Choose between them on whether the response can reach a boundary, not on
whether it happens to in the sample: the two ask different questions, and
\code{ordbeta()} fitted to a response with no boundary observations leaves its
cutpoints with nothing to identify them.

\code{tweedie()} is the compound Poisson-gamma, for a non-negative response with a
point mass at zero and a continuous positive part, which is the shape of
spending, rainfall, insurance claims and earnings. It is the analogue of
\code{ordbeta()} at the other end: one predictor again drives both parts, but
through the mean rather than through a cutpoint, since \eqn{\mu = \exp(\eta)}
and \eqn{\mathrm{Var}(y) = \phi\mu^p} together fix the probability of a zero
at \eqn{\exp(-\mu^{2-p}/(\phi(2-p)))}. That is what makes it a single process
and is also its restriction: the share of zeros has no level of its own, so a
response whose zeros are more or less common than its mean implies wants a
two-part model instead, which \code{zi_poisson()} and \code{zi_negbin()} are for counts
and which \code{custom_family()} can supply for anything else.

Two things follow from the mean being \eqn{\exp(\eta)} exactly. A
counterfactual mean through \CRANpkg{marginaleffects} needs nothing beyond the
forest, unlike a two-part model where it has to be recombined across
predictors; and the fit is comparable with a \code{poisson()} or \code{Gamma("log")} fit
of the same response, since all three put the same quantity on the same
scale.
}

\subsection{Several Additive Predictors}{

Most families model one parameter with one forest. Some model several, and
then every argument that could mean something different for each of them may
be given per forest, keyed by the names below or positionally;
\code{\link[=bartisan_control]{bartisan_control()}} states the recycling rule and lists which arguments it
covers, and \code{formula} is among them, so a forest can have predictors of its
own.

The first forest is always the main parameter, the one a single-forest family
would have on its own. This table is the canonical list of the names, which
\code{vignette("families")} reproduces:\tabular{ll}{
   Family \tab Forests, in order \cr
   \code{gaussian_ls()} \tab \code{mean}, \code{log_sd} \cr
   \code{Gamma_ls()} \tab \code{mean}, \code{log_dispersion} \cr
   \code{zi_poisson()}, \code{zi_negbin()} \tab \code{count}, \code{zero} \cr
   \code{custom_family(num_predictors = k)} \tab \code{eta1} ... \code{etak} \cr
   \code{multinomial("logit")} \tab one per level, or per non-reference level \cr
   \code{multinomial("probit")} \tab one per non-reference level, named for its contrast \cr
   everything else \tab \code{eta} \cr
}


\code{mean} is the mean and \code{log_sd} is the logarithm of the standard deviation,
which is the scale the forest works on. \code{Gamma_ls()}'s \code{mean} is the log mean,
as \code{Gamma("log")}'s single forest is, and its \code{log_dispersion} is the
logarithm of the dispersion, so that in both location-scale families a larger
second predictor means more spread. \code{count} is the linear predictor of the
count component and \code{zero} that of the inflation component. A custom family's
nuisance parameters are not on this list: they are carried as trailing forests
pinned to a single leaf, and nothing about them is set per forest.

\strong{A forest whose formula names no predictor is a constant.} \code{~ 1} leaves that
forest nothing to split on, so every tree in it is a stump and the parameter is
one drawn scalar. Every family here that takes more than one formula accepts
that, which is what makes the distinction between a nuisance parameter and an
empty forest a thin one: \code{gaussian_ls()} with \code{~ 1} on its scale is
\code{gaussian()}, \code{Gamma_ls()} with \code{~ 1} is \code{Gamma("log")}, and \code{zi_poisson()}
with \code{~ 1} on its inflation part is the zero-inflated Poisson with a single
structural-zero probability. Note that the scalar is drawn under the leaf prior
rather than under the prior the corresponding built-in family puts on its
nuisance parameter, so the two agree closely rather than exactly. The
multinomial families are the exception, for the reason given below.

So, for a location-scale model with a smaller scale forest and a restricted
set of predictors for it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{bartisan(list(y ~ x1 + x2 + x3, log_sd = ~ x1), data = d,
         family = gaussian_ls(), num_trees = c(mean = 50, log_sd = 10))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{The multinomial families are the exception.} Their forests are the levels of
one categorical parameter and act together rather than describing separate
components of the response distribution, so there is nothing a caller could
mean by giving one level a different prior or a different set of predictors
from another. Every argument applies to all of their forests at once, and more
than one value is an error rather than a silent recycling.
}

\subsection{Supplying a Likelihood}{

\code{custom_family()} takes the log density itself, as an R function, and fits the
model that goes with it. Nothing else about the sampler changes: the
leaf-level Laplace proposal needs the first two derivatives of the log density
with respect to each additive predictor and nothing more, and central
differences of the supplied function produce both.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# A Poisson model written out by hand. Terms free of eta may be dropped;
# they cancel from every acceptance ratio.
pois <- custom_family(function(y, eta) y * eta[, 1] - exp(eta[, 1]),
                      start = log(mean(d$y)))

# Two predictors: a mean and a log standard deviation.
ls <- custom_family(function(y, eta) dnorm(y, eta[, 1], exp(eta[, 2]),
                                           log = TRUE),
                    num_predictors = 2, start = c(0, 0))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The function is called once per leaf per Fisher-scoring step with the
observations reaching that leaf, so it must be vectorized over \code{y} and the
rows of \code{eta}; it must not be vectorized \emph{within} an observation, and it must
return exactly one value per row. Supplying \code{derivatives} cuts three calls to
one and removes the differencing error.

Nuisance parameters are drawn alongside the trees when \code{aux_names} names
them, and \code{logdens} then takes a third argument holding their current values:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# A Gaussian written out by hand, with its scale drawn rather than fixed.
by_hand <- custom_family(
  logdens = function(y, eta, aux) dnorm(y, eta[, 1], exp(aux[1]), log = TRUE),
  aux_names = "log_sigma", aux_start = 0)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

They are reported in \code{fit$aux} under those names, and covered by \code{summary()}
and \code{\link[=diagnose]{diagnose()}} like any other family's. There is no prior argument and no
bounds argument, because a nuisance parameter here is carried as an additive
predictor whose forest is pinned at depth zero (one tree that can never
split, so the forest is a single scalar), and it is drawn by the same
Laplace-plus-Metropolis step as any leaf, under that step's Gaussian leaf
prior. So a parameter with a restricted range is handled the way it would be
for a real predictor, by writing the transform into \code{logdens}: the \code{exp()}
above is what keeps the scale positive.

What \code{custom_family()} does not do: the response must be numeric, so a factor
has to be coded first; and since the package cannot know what the mean of the
density is, \code{predict(type = "response")} returns the additive predictors
rather than a fitted mean.
}
}
\examples{
data("rhc")
set.seed(123)

# A right-censored response, given as the time and the event indicator,
# with the error distribution estimated rather than assumed
fit <- bartisan(cbind(days, death) ~ ., data = rhc, family = dpm_aft(),
                num_trees = 10, num_burn = 50, num_draws = 50)

# The shape the errors came out
head(error_density(fit))

# The same response under proportional hazards, whose predictor is a log
# hazard ratio and whose baseline is free to take any shape
bartisan(cbind(days, death) ~ ., data = rhc, family = ph(),
         num_trees = 10, num_burn = 50, num_draws = 50)

# An unordered response, with one forest per category and a prior that is
# symmetric in them
bartisan(race ~ . - days - death, data = rhc, family = multinomial(),
         num_trees = 10, num_burn = 50, num_draws = 50)

# A link the engine does not compile, applied from R
bartisan(death ~ . - days, data = rhc, family = binomial("cauchit"),
         num_trees = 10, num_burn = 50, num_draws = 50)

}
